      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "AIMS",
          "content": "The purpose of the study was to investigate the effects of music on pain reaction and anxiety during labour."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "Music therapy has been used on clinical medicine. Only few scientific studies validate the value on labour women."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "DESIGN",
          "content": "Randomised controlled trial."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "Sixty primiparas expected to have a normal spontaneous delivery were randomly assigned to either the experimental group (n = 30) or the control group (n = 30). The experimental group received routine care and music therapy, whereas the control group received routine care only. A self-report visual analogue scale for pain and a nurse-rated present behavioural intensity were used to measure labour pain. Anxiety was measured with a visual analogue scale for anxiety and finger temperature. Pain and anxiety between groups were compared during the latent phase (2-4 cm cervical dilation) and active phase (5-7 cm) separately."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Our results revealed that compared with the control group, the experimental group had significantly lower pain, anxiety and a higher finger temperature during the latent phase of labour. However, no significant differences were found between the two groups on all outcome measures during the active phase."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "This study provides evidence for the use of music as an empirically based intervention of women for labour pain and anxiety during the latent phase of labour."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "RELEVANCE TO CLINICAL PRACTICE",
          "content": "The findings support that music listening is an acceptable and non-medical coping strategy for labouring women. Especially, apply in reducing the pain and anxiety for women who are at the early phase of labour."
        }
      ]},
